WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.350
Thank you so much to my 15.280
students for nominating me

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.200
for this Teaching with
Digital Technology award.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.650
We are obviously living
at a very important time

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:36.850
for higher education.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.250
And we have collectively,
the opportunity

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.370
to reinvent how we're
delivering that type of content.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:46.180
And I'm very much looking
forward to understanding

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.250
how collectively we find
new ways of delivering

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.570
technological content
to our students

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.610
to empower them to
use them in practice

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:01.210
but also to enhance their
educational experience.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.200
The materials that we explore
and the sense of community

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.990
that we foster provides us
with the space and opportunity

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.080
to showcase our care
for one another.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:21.340
And so once we had to face
the tragedy of COVID-19,

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.780
the means to express
our care and our joy

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:31.390
for being together
changed, but the foundation

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.070
remains the same.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.330
My main approach to
teaching is really

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.560
oversharing in the interest
of building community.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:41.600
I think that there can be no
real learning without trust.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.230
And there can be no
trust without sharing

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:45.550
our own vulnerabilities.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:49.180
And it just so happened that
the week of the campus closure,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.600
we were discussing the
importance of connectedness

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.900
to place for human well-being.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.550
So I immediately
asked the students

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.280
to think about what
it feels like when

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.070
the place that you
feel most connected to

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.720
is wrested from you.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.870
Could this help us
empathize with the plight

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.630
of displaced peoples in
the world, for instance?

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:15.580
I wanted the students to
be anthropologists studying

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:21.250
this strange, new reality and
for that spirit of curiosity

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.790
to empower them and
to give them strength.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.470
The different life
circumstances out

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:30.520
of which we make meaning, what
we often call our backgrounds,

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.220
a fitting metaphor
perhaps for the Zoom era,

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.750
has now come ever
to the foreground.

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.850
Remote teaching
was a heartbreak,

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.760
though it had its
unexpected boons as speaking

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.940
with students fixed in
equally-sized video boxes made

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.720
our discussions more democratic
though also made clear because

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.180
of the absence of
students unable reliably

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.260
to connect how far we have to
go in building this commons,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.590
a fact highlighting the real
danger in which US and world

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.470
democracy now find
themselves, a peril that

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.840
must see us confront
racial injustice,

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.510
digital division, public
health catastrophe,

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.280
and the rise of unreason.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.217
We threw away all
or most of our plans

00:03:11.217 --> 00:03:13.050
in the interest of
getting the best learning

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.240
experience to our students.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.640
When we first got word
that this semester was

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.920
going to be online,
one of the thoughts

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.040
that went through
my head was how

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.780
is a two-inch high,
two-dimensional version of me

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.090
possibly going to
compete with all

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:35.410
of the other things that are
on student's computer screens.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.250
The first time I set
up a graph, I actually

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.760
walked off screen and then did
a little computer trickery.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.950
And it looked like
I walked inside

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.850
of the graph inside of my slide.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.500
Luckily, MIT has great
resources in digital learning,

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.480
such as the videotaping
rooms and the MITx system.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.400
When the class was
moved online, I

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.270
decided to use OneNote
as my virtual blackboard.

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:01.620
It's quite easy to use.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.290
Writing and drawing on
it feel really smooth.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.840
Catsoop is free and
open source software.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.060
It's freely available online.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:11.250
And over the past
several semesters,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.620
we've seen a pretty
steady increase

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.600
in the number of
subjects using it at MIT.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.959
And sometimes,
these are subjects

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:21.269
that are very different
from the ones that I teach.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:25.708
And so it's been extra
neat to see the things

00:04:25.708 --> 00:04:27.750
that they're doing with
the software and the ways

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.820
that they're extending it.

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.850
As well as reproducing
all of the key features

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:36.130
of in-person lectures and office
hours as much as possible,

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.160
the other guiding
principle of flexibility

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.860
meant making the course
content as accessible

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.810
as possible in as many
formats as possible.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:46.020
This is a very hands-on class.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.350
We set the foundation by
going through the calculations

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.360
that an astronomer
will make in order

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.690
to determine what exposure
time they should use when

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:56.370
at the telescope.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.020
The students were
able to do this

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.660
for the target of their
choice and then submit

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:04.350
this as a request to the
Elliot 24-inch telescope

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:05.910
at Wallace Observatory.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.880
Although they weren't there
for the actual experiments,

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.020
by watching the videos
and then talking with me

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.600
and listening to my
presentations in the lab times

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:18.180
and then meeting
with us individually

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.300
about their spectra, they
were able to back this

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.420
up and talk about their
peptide to each other and us.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.510
A one-on-one Zoom conference
is very productive

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:31.200
and has the advantage that the
student can record the session

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.773
so that if we talk
and say something,

00:05:33.773 --> 00:05:35.190
and they forget
what we said, they

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.630
can go back and
look at it again,

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.790
which is not what happens
in person when they're

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:40.530
meeting with us in the lab.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.130
So I have to admit that when
I got the email about winning

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:47.430
a Digital Technology
Teaching Award,

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.520
I thought it was
a joke or a scam

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:55.320
because I am famously
clueless with technology.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.630
I have to get my
students to set it up

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:03.150
if I need a class e-list or a
Google Doc or what have you.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:06.210
We also use technology
to travel virtually

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.830
when we couldn't
do it in real life,

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:12.930
for example, by walking city
streets in Google View mode,

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:16.910
visiting museum rooms,
browsing restaurant menus.

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:20.040
For some sections of the
course, I flipped the classroom.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.260
What this means
is that I recorded

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.570
lectures, narrated some slides
and sent those out in advance.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:29.340
And then during the synchronous
remote class time period

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:34.170
on Zoom, we use that time
to have more discussions,

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:36.670
mini cases, work out problems.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.670
And we also discussed
the real-time changes

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:40.920
that were happening in the
US tax code in response

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:42.000
to the pandemic.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.890
Virtual class
discussions just tend

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:45.680
to stall at unexpected moments.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:47.430
And when that happened,
what helped for me

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.230
was to use breakout rooms.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:50.820
Learning objectives
can be achieved

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:52.560
in many different ways.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.870
We might be used to
achieving a learning

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.090
objective in a
particular way that's

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:58.800
been done for many years.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.680
I think the transition
to online teaching

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.460
has made us take a step back and
acknowledge that perhaps there

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.010
are multiple ways to
get at that objective.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.380
I designed a variety of
games anywhere from bingo

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:11.910
to Battleship.

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.680
Some ideas worked
better than others,

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.810
but students embraced
these experiments

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.600
with incredible
flexibility and good humor.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.030
I've been asked what
did we do to create

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:23.820
appealing and
thought-provoking lectures?

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:25.230
Here are some tips.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:28.500
1, lectures are stories.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.440
2, design for the media.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.220
We're used to seeing videos
with cuts every 2.5 seconds.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:36.600
Keep it moving.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.630
And 3, it's OK to have fun.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.510
Lots of the activities
in my classes

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.990
are role-play
simulations or scenarios

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:50.370
that put students
in specific contexts

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:55.050
that mimic real-world or
professional practice contexts.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:58.410
Ask them to use what they've
been reading and talking about,

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:00.510
and then give each
other feedback.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.880
And then we often
video what they're

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.800
doing so they can see how
they're presenting themselves

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.410
to other people in
other people's eyes.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:17.070
I really appreciated and enjoyed
the discussions via Zoom.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:21.240
The students were really willing
to engage and ask questions.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:26.610
And I felt we could create not
quite, but almost a classroom

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:27.750
atmosphere.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.270
We've learned in this
digital teaching environment

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.630
to bring Socratic
dialogue, which

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:37.549
allows students to be the agents
who educate each other and us,

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:38.909
the instructors.

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:41.210
It's been a very powerful
way of teaching students

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.070
how to discover for themselves.

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:44.750
I can't thank you
enough for this.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:46.070
I'll never forget our class.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:48.615
I'll never forget
the spring of 2020.

00:08:48.615 --> 00:08:51.350
And I look forward to see
you all on campus again.

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:54.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

